// example_fnptr_bad_context.mir — REJECTION case documenting the diagnostic CHANGE that came with
// function-name decay (see example_fnptr_inferred).
//
// A bare function name in value position used to be rejected outright with
// "'f' is a function; did you mean to call it?", which doubled as a forgotten-'()' hint. Now that
// the name decays to its function-pointer type so ':=' and inferred default parameter values can
// work, that hint is gone: a forgotten '()' surfaces as an ordinary type error at the point of
// use instead. This fixture pins the replacement wording, so the change stays deliberate and
// visible rather than becoming an accident.
//
// Here 'double + 1' is arithmetic on a function pointer, which is rejected on its own terms.
//
// CAVEAT worth knowing: not every forgotten '()' is still caught. A function pointer is
// boolean-coercible as a nil test (a pre-existing rule — 'const g: fn(i32) -> i32 = double;
// if g {}' has always compiled), so a forgotten '()' in a CONDITION — 'if double {}' — now
// silently means "if double != nil", which is always true, where it used to be an error. That is
// the price of decay; nothing here can detect it, because the decayed expression is well-typed.
//
// Expected: compile error — "arithmetic is not allowed on function pointer types", NOT
// "did you mean to call it?".

fn double(v: i32) -> i32 {
    return v * 2
}

pub fn main() -> i32 {
    return double + 1
}
